## Engineering principles (non-negotiable)

Whatever the mechanisms in `docs/*` say, these hold. Where the scope is limited, say so in the entry.

<!-- Tag each entry "enforced by <gate>" or "review-only" — the review-only column is the
     standing pool of guardrail candidates. Draw on these per project, preferring few and
     accurate over many and hollow. Shape: conclusion (bold) + concrete form + why + link. -->

- **TDD/BDD first — the failing test before any change to observable behaviour.** Describe it as `Given/When/Then` or `describe`/`it`, covering the happy path plus at least one edge or failure case **the contract genuinely owns** (the criterion is the gate in [`docs/testing.md`](docs/testing.md#covering-the-behaviour-space-deliberately) — a contract with no edge gets only the happy path, not a manufactured one). The two exceptions are in [`docs/testing.md`](docs/testing.md#exceptions-to-tdd) and are **not** a blanket rule by file or task category.

- **Confirm the bug really exists before fixing it.** Reproduce it **with a failure reason matching the report**, fix the reproduction into a failing test, then change the implementation. When you cannot reproduce it, **say so plainly and stop** — do not fix a hallucinated bug.

- **Fix the root cause, do not patch.** No guard at the consumer to paper over a bad value from the producer; normalise once at the boundary, not repeatedly at call sites. Refactoring **the unit you are changing** is in scope — a different thing from the drive-by refactoring banned below.

- **Scope discipline — touch only what this task needs.** One change = the producer + its tests + at most one drive-by drift fix. **Banned**: smuggled-in refactors, rename sweeps, formatting, import reordering, unrelated test changes — they drown the real change and break `git bisect`. Unrelated mess gets **reported, not fixed in passing**. Three lines of similar code beat a premature abstraction.

- **Reuse first — grep before writing.** Two parallel implementations drift within weeks. Decidable heuristics: a new file importing both a UI primitive **and** a data store is probably rebuilding <existing component>; copying more than 10 lines → extract it; the same fix in two similar blocks → **the second is a bug**, delete it and call the first. <This repository's shared entry points: components, hooks, utilities.>

- **SOLID / high cohesion, low coupling — on this repository's own extension points.**
  - <Adding an X → implement `<interface name>` and `Register()` it in `init()`; **do not `switch` on a type string in shared code**.>
  - <Depend on interfaces through a getter; a service must not import another service's repository.>
  - <Dependency direction is one-way: `A → B → C`; `<cross-cutting layer>` is a leaf.>
  *<Enforced by `<check name>` since `<date>` (inside `<command>`); the exemption list **only shrinks**.>*

- **No defensive code — a pointless fallback is a liability.** Defences for the impossible, swallowed errors and shims for retired data become noise nobody dares delete, while the real bug hides behind the guard.
  - **Validate at boundaries only** — <this repository's boundaries: external input / IPC / plugin host calls>. Internal calls trust each other.
  - **Do not swallow errors** — `catch { return defaultState }` masks failure and propagates dirty state.
  - **Do not double-fallback a user-configurable field** — `value || "default"` overrides a deliberate empty value.
  - **Do not leave runtime shims for retired data** — a migration runs once, so delete the field.

- **Direct replacement beats an adapter sandwich.** When swapping a backend or library, **replace in place**; no `interface Foo + LegacyImpl + NewImpl` unless both coexist at runtime. A sandwich forces every call site to work out which layer it is on.

- **Leave no dead code and no deletion markers.** Git remembers; delete it outright.

- **Before adding a dependency, ask whether what exists can do it.** A permanent maintenance and security burden. When genuinely needed: lockfile committed, one package manager only (<enforcement mechanism>), and the PR explains why the existing approach was insufficient.

- **Credentials and sensitive data stay out of the repository, the logs and the reports.** Secrets in `.env` (gitignored) or <secret management>. Redact before pasting anywhere. <This project's data red lines.>

- **Critical paths must be observable.** <External calls / state changes / permission decisions / failure degradations> emit structured logs with a correlation id, so a failure can be reconstructed rather than guessed at. Details in [`docs/observability.md`](docs/observability.md).

